.TH CYAML_DICTIONARY_NEXT 3 "2026-09-29" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
cyaml_dictionary_next \- step a dictionary cursor onto the next member
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cyaml.h>

bool cyaml_dictionary_next(cyaml_dictionary_iter *it);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Moves the cursor it, which cyaml_dictionary_first(3) filled, to the
member that comes after the current one in insertion order.  After a
call that returns true, it->key and it->value describe that member.
The cursor has held that member since it reached the current one, so
any change that leaves that successor in place keeps it valid: a
replacement of any value, the removal of any other member, and an
insert.  Removing the successor or destroying the dictionary
invalidates it.  cyaml_dictionary_first(3) describes the order, how
long it->key and it->value live, and each of those changes.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
it  A cursor that cyaml_dictionary_first(3) filled.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
true when it stands on the next member.

false when the previous call gave the last member, and on every call
after that, and the function then sets it->key and it->value to
NULL.  Also false when it is NULL.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Removing the current member with cyaml_dictionary_remove(3) leaves
the cursor valid, but removing the successor or destroying the
dictionary doesn't.  cyaml_dictionary_first(3) lists every change and
its effect.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cyaml_dictionary_first (3),
.BR cyaml_dictionary_remove (3)
